## Opening the Export dialog

Press `Ctrl+Shift+E`, or open *Work ▸ Export*. The submenu names what you have open:
*Export Book*, *Export Part*, *Export Chapter*, *Export Scene*, *Export Note*,
*Export Folder*. Each opens the dialog set to that container. *Choose…*, and
`Ctrl+Shift+E` itself, open it on a checkbox tree of your whole binder instead.

## Choosing what to export

The *What to export* column offers the container you arrived with, or *Custom selection*:
a checkbox tree of your whole binder.

- The tree opens with your prose already checked. Uncheck what you want left out.
- Checking a chapter checks its scenes. A partly checked chapter shows a dash.
- Items whose Inspector *Include in exports* switch is off are hidden.
  *Show non-exportable* reveals them, and checking one there counts for this export only.

## Picking a format

*Format* offers:

- *Word* (`.docx`) and *LibreOffice* (`.odt`), which carry comments
- *PDF* and *EPUB*, for a finished book
- *HTML*, *Markdown*, *Djot*, *Text* and *LaTeX*, all plain files

## Choosing a style

*Style* is a saved bundle of choices that shapes the output whichever format you pick:

- chapter and part headings, numbered or not
- scene breaks, line spacing and justification
- which of notes, synopses, scene titles, epigraphs and paratexts go in
- footnotes: whether they go in, and how they are numbered
- whether your comments go out with the file
- where a new page starts

A style applies wherever the format can honour it.

## Making a style of your own

Built-in styles cannot be edited. To make your own, open
*Settings ▸ Compile & Export ▸ Export Formats* and choose *Duplicate* on a built-in. The
copy appears in the same list, after the built-in ones, and *Edit* opens it.

## Where the file goes

*Destination* is the file to write. Type a path, or pick one with the folder button beside
it. Changing the format changes the extension for you. If that file already exists,
Skribisto asks before replacing it.

## Running the export

Click *Export*.

The export runs in the background, and a message follows it:

- While it runs, a *Cancel* button stops it.
- When the file lands, the message names it and offers *Open* and *Show in folder*.

## Getting a document back

- With the style's *Include comments* on, `.docx` and `.odt` carry each comment out
  anchored to its passage.
- Re-importing that file returns the comments to their places. That relies on the style's
  *Include round-trip markers*, which starts on. See [the round trip](:help-round-trip) for how
  that works.
- Markdown and plain text come back through [importing documents](:help-import-documents),
  but with no comments.
